{"name":"Ocean City","hex":"#7896ba","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Ocean City","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A placename:\nA resort city in Cape May County, New Jersey, United States.","A resort city in Cape May County, New Jersey, United States."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Ocean_City"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ocean City, Maryland","blocks":[{"text":"Ocean City is a resort city in Worcester County, Maryland, United States, along the Atlantic coast of the United States. The population was 6,844 in the 2020 U.S. census, although during summer weekends the city hosts between 320,000 and 345,000 vacationers, and up to eight million visitors annually. During the summer, Ocean City becomes the second most populated municipality in Maryland, after Baltimore. It is part of the Salisbury metropolitan area as defined by the United States Census Bureau."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/6/65/Ocean_City_Maryland_USA_2018-07-14.png/330px-Ocean_City_Maryland_USA_2018-07-14.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocean_City%2C_Maryland","wikidata":"Q687905"},{"title":"Ocean City, New Jersey","blocks":[{"text":"Ocean City is a city in Cape May County, in the U.S. state of New Jersey. It is the principal city of the Ocean City metropolitan statistical area, which encompasses all of Cape May County, and is part of the Philadelphia metropolitan area. It is part of the South Jersey region of the state."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Ocean_City_NJ_beach_looking_south_from_Music_Pier.jpeg/330px-Ocean_City_NJ_beach_looking_south_from_Music_Pier.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocean_City%2C_New_Jersey","wikidata":"Q1017603"},{"title":"Ocean","blocks":[{"text":"The ocean is the body of salt water that covers approximately 70.8% of Earth. The ocean is conventionally divided into large bodies of water, which are also referred to as oceans, and are themselves mostly divided into seas, gulfs, and subsequent bodies of water. The ocean contains 97% of Earth's water and is the primary component of Earth's hydrosphere, acting as a huge reservoir of heat for Earth's energy budget, as well as for its carbon cycle and water cycle, forming the basis for climate and weather patterns worldwide. The ocean is essential to life on Earth, harboring most of Earth's animals and protist life. It originated photosynthesis and therefore Earth's atmospheric oxygen, and still supplies half of it."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Pacific_Ocean_as_viewed_from_GOES-18_on_September_23%2C_2023.jpg/330px-Pacific_Ocean_as_viewed_from_GOES-18_on_September_23%2C_2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocean","wikidata":"Q9430"},{"title":"City","blocks":[{"text":"A city is a human settlement of a substantial size. The term \"city\" has different meanings around the world and in some places the settlement can be very small. Even where the term is limited to larger settlements, there is no universally agreed definition of the lower numerical boundary for their size. In a more specific sense, a city can be defined as a permanent and densely populated place with administratively defined boundaries whose members work primarily on non-agricultural tasks. Cities generally have extensive systems for housing, transportation, sanitation, utilities, land use, production of goods, and communication. Their density facilitates interaction between people, government organizations, and businesses, sometimes benefiting different parties in the process, such as improving the efficiency of goods and service distribution."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Tokyo_Shibuya_Scramble_Crossing_2018-10-09.jpg/330px-Tokyo_Shibuya_Scramble_Crossing_2018-10-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City","wikidata":"Q515"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Seal_of_Ocean_City%2C_Maryland.png/500px-Seal_of_Ocean_City%2C_Maryland.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Seal_of_Ocean_City%2C_Maryland.png/1280px-Seal_of_Ocean_City%2C_Maryland.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Seal_of_Ocean_City%2C_Maryland.png/500px-Seal_of_Ocean_City%2C_Maryland.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASeal_of_Ocean_City%2C_Maryland.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Oceancitymd_inlet.JPG/500px-Oceancitymd_inlet.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Oceancitymd_inlet.JPG/1280px-Oceancitymd_inlet.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Oceancitymd_inlet.JPG/500px-Oceancitymd_inlet.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ocean City's inlet during the offseason","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOceancitymd_inlet.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Maryland_-_Ocean_City_-_NARA_-_23941153_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Maryland_-_Ocean_City_-_NARA_-_23941153_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Maryland_-_Ocean_City_-_NARA_-_23941153_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280p